<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The only lint configuration in this repository, and it exists for one measured reason.

  Android Lint's AndroidAutoDetector turns on as soon as an application declares itself an Android
  Auto media app - the com.google.android.gms.car.application meta-data plus a res/xml descriptor
  whose uses element names "media", both of which :app now has. It then looks for the service and
  the intent filters an Auto media app needs. It looks for them in THIS MODULE'S OWN manifest source
  files, and MuPlay's playback service is deliberately declared in :core:media's manifest instead
  (read that file's header: the service class lives there, and a second application module must get
  the declaration without anyone remembering to copy it).

  So the detector reports both of its errors against an application that satisfies neither check as
  far as it can see, while the artifact that actually ships satisfies one of them completely.

  MEASURED, in both directions, rather than reasoned about:

    * The merged manifest DOES carry the browse action. AGP's own merged debug manifest
      (app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/) contains
      android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" inside MuPlaybackService's
      intent-filter, and verifyDebugManifest fails the build when it does not.
      MissingMediaBrowserServiceIntentFilter reports it missing anyway.
    * The detector does not read library manifests. Adding
      android:name="android.media.action.MEDIA_PLAY_FROM_SEARCH" to the same service in
      :core:media's manifest and re-running :app:lintDebug left MissingIntentFilterForMediaSearch
      reported, unchanged. So that check cannot go green in this layout either, whatever the
      declaration says - it is not currently telling the truth about a gap, it is reporting the
      only answer it is able to give.

  Both are therefore checks that cannot pass, not checks that found something. A gate whose verdict
  does not depend on the thing it claims to check is the defect class this repository exists to keep
  out of its own gates, and leaving it on would train everyone to ignore a red lint.

  WHAT REPLACES THEM, and it is stronger evidence, not weaker:

    * MissingMediaBrowserServiceIntentFilter -> `verifyDebugManifest` / `verifyReleaseManifest`
      require android:name="android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService" (and the Media3 browse action,
      and the car meta-data, and the descriptor resource) in AGP's own MERGED manifest, for both
      variants, on every `check`. See AUTOMOTIVE_DECLARATIONS in AndroidApplicationConventionPlugin.
      `verifyAutomotiveDescriptor` then reads the descriptor resource itself, which no manifest
      check of any kind can do.
    * MissingIntentFilterForMediaSearch -> NOT replaced by an equivalent, and that is stated rather
      than hidden. Voice search is Plan 5 Task 6's work: the intent filter and its handler in
      MuPlaybackService.onStartCommand land together. The one gate on it is `ConventionTest`'s
      `a declared play-from-search filter must have a handler and a gate entry`, which holds the
      filter, the handler and the merged-manifest entry to a single answer so that no two of the
      three can land without the third.

  `ConventionTest`'s `no Android Auto lint check is disabled without a named replacement` holds this
  file to that: an id may be listed here only if the test names what took over from it, and the test
  checks that the replacement is really there.
-->
<lint>
  <issue id="MissingMediaBrowserServiceIntentFilter" severity="ignore" />
  <issue id="MissingIntentFilterForMediaSearch" severity="ignore" />
</lint>
